# 1. Purpose

The purpose of the Foundation is to build the technical infrastructure required to operate an AI Software Engineering Company.

The system must coordinate eight independent software engineering specialists while preserving:

- Company identity.
- Project information.
- Business requirements.
- Technical architecture.
- Engineering decisions.
- Repository history.
- Task ownership.
- Execution history.
- Quality evidence.
- Failures and recovery state.
- Security and approval policies.

The Foundation must reduce unnecessary dependence on long ChatGPT conversations.

A new AI session or authorized worker must be able to reconstruct relevant project context from persistent, verified company records.

This is an engineering target. It must not be described as implemented until automated acceptance tests prove it.

# 2. Product Identity and Boundaries

The product is an AI Software Engineering Company OS.

It is not:

- A single generic chatbot.
- A collection of passive Markdown role descriptions.
- A simple prompt-routing wrapper.
- An unrestricted autonomous shell executor.
- A replacement for independent software testing.
- A system that treats generated code as verified code.

The product must support independent engineering ownership and shared organizational coordination.

## 2.1 Company ownership

The owner controls:

- Company strategy.
- Project priorities.
- Business requirements.
- Risk acceptance.
- Production approvals.
- Critical architecture decisions.
- Spending and provider policies.

An Owner Advocate capability must evaluate proposed decisions against these requirements and identify technical and business risks.

It must not bypass owner approval.

## 2.2 Project, tenant and workspace separation

Every project must have independent identity, requirements, architecture, memory, repository references, tasks, decisions, artifacts, and execution history.

The Company OS must support two first-class business tracks on the same underlying workforce:

1. **Internal delivery:** projects owned by the AI-Dev-Company owner, including the company's own products, websites, applications, SaaS products, and automations.
2. **Customer delivery:** separately contracted projects owned by external customer tenants and their authorized users.

These tracks may reuse the same approved specialist definitions, model adapters, orchestration services, and platform infrastructure. They must not share private project data or execution authority.

A tenant is the top-level security and commercial boundary. A tenant may contain one or more workspaces; a workspace may contain one or more projects. The internal company workspace is not a customer workspace.

Tenant isolation must cover, at minimum:

- Project requirements, decisions, memory, task history, and context packets.
- Source repositories, branches, workspaces, generated artifacts, and backups.
- Credentials, provider access, tool sessions, and execution environments.
- Search indexes, caches, embeddings, temporary files, and retrieved documents.
- Prompts, model responses, logs, traces, test evidence, and support records.
- Usage, quotas, approvals, invoices, and billing records.

Cross-tenant access is denied by default. A shared runtime or common agent definition does not grant an agent permission to inspect another tenant's data. Shared public company policies and non-sensitive role specifications may be reused only through explicit, read-only, policy-controlled references.

The system must not reuse one customer's private source code, prompts, requirements, credentials, or deliverables for another customer's work. Customer content must not be used for model training or cross-customer reuse unless a separate explicit agreement, lawful basis, and enforceable technical policy authorize that use.

Tenant membership, data ownership, retention, export, deletion, and any legal hold must have explicit records and auditable lifecycle rules. Deletion must include defined treatment of replicas and backups; a deletion request must not be represented as complete while retained copies remain outside the declared retention policy.

AI-Dev-Company must remain independent from Intellect Media.

## 2.3 Customer and commercial service boundary

The future customer-facing platform must support:

- Customer onboarding and tenant creation.
- Workspace and project creation.
- Customer membership, roles, invitations, and access revocation.
- Project requirements, approvals, milestones, and delivery status.
- Deliverable review, acceptance, revision requests, and handover.
- Service plans, quotas, metering, usage records, and billing.
- Support requests, incidents, and project communication.
- Customer offboarding, export, retention, and deletion workflows.

Billing and customer-facing screens may be delivered in a later platform phase. Their identity, data ownership, metering, and authorization contracts must be designed before the execution system is allowed to process real customer workloads.

