# 17. Definition of Done

A Foundation component is complete only when its relevant acceptance criteria are implemented, automated where possible, and supported by recorded evidence.

| Area | Acceptance criterion |
|---|---|
| Company identity | One canonical identity across services |
| Tenant identity | Each customer/internal tenant has a canonical scope and lifecycle |
| Tenant isolation | Negative tests reject cross-tenant reads, writes, retrieval, tool use, artifacts, and logs |
| Project isolation | No accidental cross-project data or credential access |
| Agent identity | Exactly eight canonical specialist identities; manifest validation is not runtime activation |
| Agent readiness | Runtime, permissions, tools, contracts, and domain evaluations pass before Active |
| Memory | Persistent, versioned, tenant-scoped, and recoverable |
| Decision history | Source, author/actor, approval state, version, and timestamp retained |
| Context | Correct tenant/project/specialist retrieval with provenance |
| Context trust | Stale, conflicting, untrusted, and prompt-injection content cannot override policy |
| Token management | Budgets enforced with omissions reported and provider estimates labelled correctly |
| Repository intelligence | Reproducible source snapshots and dependency evidence |
| Incremental analysis | Stale or tampered cache evidence rejected |
| Audit | Findings linked to commit, task, attempt, context, toolchain, and test evidence |
| Task execution | Valid state transitions, idempotency, bounded retries, and recovery |
| Handoffs | Versioned payload, ownership acknowledgement, evidence, and permission scope |
| Concurrency | Conflicting writes are isolated or rejected; stale workers cannot publish |
| Provider gateway | Capability-aware routing, data-policy enforcement, usage metering, and tested failures |
| Tool security | Sandbox, allowlists, secret controls, resource limits, and negative tests |
| Governance | Scoped, expiring, revocable approvals and fail-closed authorization |
| Quality | Measurable project-risk-specific gates and independent verification |
| Observability | End-to-end traces, operational metrics, and alerts with sensitive-data redaction |
| Disaster recovery | Encrypted backups, restore drills, defined RPO/RTO, and tested recovery |
| Customer delivery | Tenant onboarding, approval, delivery, metering, billing, support, and offboarding are verified before commercial launch |

The complete Foundation must support a new authorized development session without depending on the entire previous ChatGPT conversation.

A component must not be marked complete solely because a file, class, manifest, database table, API route, or architecture document exists. Acceptance requires behaviour, tests, and relevant operational/security evidence.

# 18. Architecture Decisions and Constraints

The following are the initial engineering decisions:

1. Python is the initial implementation language.
2. GitHub main is the source of truth for code.
3. SQLite is the current registry and memory prototype storage.
4. PostgreSQL is the future shared-state target.
5. Repository scanning must not execute scanned source.
6. Metadata cache reuse is not proof of content integrity.
7. Provider calls must be controlled and budget-aware.
8. Specialist logic remains independently owned.
9. Security and governance precede unrestricted agent execution.
10. Every completed feature requires implementation and test evidence.
11. Internal and customer workloads may share approved workforce infrastructure, but tenant data and execution authority remain isolated.
12. Tenant scope is mandatory on every customer-owned task, memory record, context packet, artifact, tool session, approval, usage record, and audit event.
13. Provider fallback may not weaken data-processing, capability, or security requirements.
14. Model output and retrieved repository/customer content are untrusted until validated; they cannot grant permissions or override policy.
15. Delivery is designed for duplicate-safe execution through idempotency and reconciliation; exactly-once behaviour must not be assumed.
16. Customer billing, support, onboarding, and offboarding use canonical tenant/project records rather than a parallel identity store.
17. No production customer workload is permitted before tenant isolation, sandbox, approval, restore, and incident-response gates pass.

Future technology changes require explicit architectural justification and migration planning.

